\begin{document}
\title{Forged in fragments: Population III star formation in Milky Way progenitor halo}

\author{Vasilii Pustovoit\orcidlink{0000-0000-0000-0000}}
\email{vpustovoit@cita.utoronto.ca}
\affiliation{Canadian Institute for Theoretical Astrophysics}
\affiliation{University of Toronto, Department of Physics}

\author{Shivan Khullar\orcidlink{0000-0000-0000-0000}}
\email{skhullar@flatironinstitute.org}
\affiliation{Your nice institute / university}
\affiliation{Your second nice institute / university}
% TO ADD: Yasmine, Phil, Norm, Mike
\author{AND COLLABORATORS\orcidlink{0000-0000-0000-0000}}
%\email{author1@you.com}
%\affiliation{Your nice institute / university}
%\affiliation{Your second nice institute / university}

\begin{abstract}
	We present a radiation-magnetohydrodynamic (RMHD) cosmological zoom-in simulation
	of Population III star formation, drawing initial conditions from the FIRE m12f
	halo at high redshift. Starting from a self-consistent cosmological environment,
	we follow the collapse of a primordial gas cloud through the formation of an
	accretion disk that becomes gravitationally unstable within $\sim 3\kyr$ of
	the first protostar's formation. Prominent spiral arms develop and fragment
	vigorously, producing $\sim 60$ sink formation events over the $\sim 15\kyr$
	of post-collapse evolution, with a total stellar mass reaching $> 150\,\Msun$.
	The stellar mass grows as $M_* \appropto t^{2}$,
	confirming the prediction of accelerating accretion in
	self-gravitating turbulent collapse,
	at rates of $\dot{M} \sim 10^{-3}$--$10^{-2}\,\Msun\,\mathrm{yr}^{-1}$.
	The disk is initially magnetically supercritical ($\mu \sim 100$--$300$)
	but the mass-to-flux ratio decreases by two orders of magnitude over
	the simulation, with the inner disk approaching and locally crossing
	the critical value ($\mu \lesssim 1$) by $15\kyr$.
	Magnetic energy grows by $\sim 4$ orders of magnitude, likely driven by
	flux-freezing compression and differential rotation.
	The star formation efficiency reaches $\sim 80\%$ within the disk
	and $\sim 50\%$ within a $0.1$ pc aperture.
	Our results demonstrate that cosmologically-realistic initial conditions,
	which produce asymmetric infall and complex angular momentum structure,
	lead to vigorous fragmentation
	\replaced{in a single, large-scale accretion disk that hosts the entire
		protostellar population and whose turbulent velocity dispersion increases
		inward, driven by gravitational collapse rather than stellar feedback.}
	{and a protostellar population whose properties differ from those found in
		earlier works.}
	\deleted{\note{can't say this because we don't have an idealized set up to compare to in this paper. You should change this to "those found in earlier works" or I would actually suggest to  remove it entirely and just talk about the properties that are different}}
\end{abstract}




\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The first generation of stars, Population~III (Population III), formed from
metal-free primordial gas in dark-matter minihaloes at redshifts
$z \sim 20$--$30$ \citep{abel02, bromm02}.
Because molecular hydrogen (H$_2$) was the only efficient gas coolant
at these epochs, the primordial gas could cool to a temperature floor of
$T \sim 200$~K at densities $n \sim 10^4\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$, before
reaching local thermodynamic equilibrium in its ro-vibrational levels
\citep{bromm02, klessen_glover23}.
The Jeans mass at this point, $M_J \sim 10^3\,\Msun$, is orders of
magnitude larger than that of present-day star-forming cores, leading
to the early expectation that Population III stars were predominantly massive
and formed in isolation within each minihalo
\citep{abel02, bromm02, yoshida08}.
These early simulations bridged cosmological collapse down to
protostellar densities ($n \sim 10^{21}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$), resolving
the characteristic stages of primordial collapse: H$_2$ three-body
formation heating at $n \gtrsim 10^8\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$,
collision-induced emission cooling at $n \gtrsim 10^{14}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$,
and a second collapse driven by H$_2$ dissociation at
$n \sim 10^{16}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ \citep{yoshida08, wollenberg20}.

This picture of isolated, monolithic Population III star formation was
overturned by simulations that continued to follow the system
after the initial protostar formed.
The infalling gas inevitably carries angular momentum, seeding a
self-gravitating accretion disk prone to gravitational instability and
fragmentation \citep{stacy10, clark11, greif11}.
Rather than a single object, a protostellar cluster arises:
\citet{greif11} found 2--5 fragments in five minihaloes within
$\sim 100$~yr of the first protostar; \citet{stacy10, stacy12} followed
systems for $\sim 5\kyr$ and found $\sim 10$ sinks with a primary
reaching $20$--$40\,\Msun$.
\citet{wollenberg20} systematically explored 45 controlled simulations
varying initial rotation and turbulence and found 5--20 sinks per halo,
with fragmentation strongly stochastic from realization to realization.
The protostellar cluster is shaped by competitive accretion
\citep{bonnell01}, fragmentation-induced starvation
\citep{wollenberg20}, protostellar mergers, and dynamical ejections,
with ejection rates of $\sim 25$--$30\%$ found across multiple studies
\citep{greif11, stacy12, wollenberg20}.

The Population III initial mass function (IMF) inferred from these simulations
is generally thought to be top-heavy, spanning sub-solar to $\gtrsim 100\,\Msun$,
but highly variable from halo to halo. \note{CITATIONS?}
\citet{hirano14} surveyed 100 minihaloes and found accretion rates of
$10^{-4}$--$10^{-2}\,\Msun\,\mathrm{yr}^{-1}$ and final masses of
$\sim 10$--$1000\,\Msun$; \citet{susa14} found masses spanning
1--$>100\,\Msun$ across several tens of halos.
When radiative feedback is included, accretion onto a single protostar
can be halted at $\sim 43\,\Msun$ \citep{hosokawa11}, though
episodic accretion from a fragmenting disk can produce intermittent
feedback and final masses from $\sim 10$ to several hundred $\Msun$
\citep{hosokawa16, stacy16}.
The large statistical survey of \citet{hirano_sakai23} across
$>100$ cosmological halos confirms that the Population III IMF is not
universal: the outcome depends on halo mass, angular momentum, and
baryon streaming velocity, and a single simulation cannot be
over-generalised.

Turbulence plays a fundamental role in shaping fragmentation and the
resulting mass distribution.
\citet{ho25} demonstrated that cosmological infall into dark-matter
potential wells universally drives supersonic turbulence in Population III
minihaloes, with Mach numbers $\mathcal{M} \sim 2$--$4$ that increase
with halo mass.
\citet{wollenberg20} confirmed that the degree of initial turbulence
strongly affects the number of fragments and the resulting mass
distribution, with higher initial turbulent velocities producing more
fragments and more stochastic outcomes.
\citet{prieto11} showed that turbulent fragmentation in a larger,
$3 \times 10^7\,\Msun$ halo at $z \sim 11$ produces $\sim 25$
gravitationally unstable clumps, proposing that infall-driven turbulence
may be the dominant fragmentation mechanism when halo masses exceed the
canonical $10^6\,\Msun$ minihalo scale.

Magnetic fields can further modify Population III star formation, though their
net effect remains debated.
Even trace cosmological seed fields are amplified during gravitational
collapse by flux-freezing and winding, driving the mass-to-flux ratio
toward criticality over timescales of a few kyr
\citep{machida08, sharda21, higashi24}.
Idealised MHD simulations suggest that strong fields can suppress disk
fragmentation \citep{hirano_machida22}, and cosmological MHD runs
by \citet{stacy22} found only two secondary sinks.
In contrast, \citet{prole22} and \citet{tanaka24} found that even
dynamically significant fields reduce but do not eliminate fragmentation.
\citet{sharda_menon25} performed the first
3D radiation-MHD Population III simulations with non-equilibrium chemistry,
turbulence, and ionising feedback, finding that magnetic fields are more
effective than radiation in limiting protostellar masses at early times,
capping the most massive star at $\sim 65\,\Msun$ within $5\kyr$
\citep[see also][]{prole25}.
These conflicting results indicate that the outcome depends sensitively
on initial field strength, field geometry, resolution, and simulation
duration \citep{klessen_glover23}.

While many Population III simulations now use cosmological initial conditions
\citep{greif11, stacy16, hirano_sakai23, latif_khochfar22, meziani26},
simulations that simultaneously combine cosmological ICs,
full radiation-MHD, and resolved individual protostellar formation remain
few. \cite{meziani26} use the FORGE'd in FIRE framework \citep{FORGE_d_in_FIRE_Hopkin_2023} to study Population III star formation in an ultra-faint dwarf galaxy progenitor, with a DM halo mass of $10^{7} M_\odot$  at z=21 and $10^{12} M_\odot$ at z=0. A minihalo embedded in a strong overdensity destined to grow to Milky Way mass may experience qualitatively different infall rates, tidal torques, and angular momentum injection compared to one in a quieter
environment destined to remain a dwarf galaxy. Therefore, in this paper, we zoom-in to the formation of the first stars in a massive halo $10^{7} M_\odot$ that eventually grows to Milky Way mass at z=0.

Whether the environmental difference between a MW-progenitor (Milky Way) and a
UFD-progenitor (Ultra Faint Dwarf) drives a qualitatively different outcome is an open question.
In this work, we address this question directly.
We perform a cosmological radiation-magnetohydrodynamic (RMHD) zoom-in
simulation of Population III star formation in the FIRE m12f initial conditions
\citep{FIRE_2, wetzel23}, which in turn were taken from the AGORA project
\citep{AGORA2014, AGORA2016}. The ICs constitute a halo destined to become a
$M_\mathrm{vir} \sim 10^{12}\,\Msun$ Milky Way analogue by $z = 0$.
We use the FORGE'd in FIRE framework \citep{FORGE_d_in_FIRE_Hopkin_2023,
	meziani26}, combining FIRE-3 \citep{FIRE_3_Updated_Hopkin} large-scale
physics with STARFORGE \citep{STARFORGE_Towa_Grudic_2021} protostellar
physics, and evolve the system for $\sim 15\kyr$ after the first
protostar forms, reaching a gas mass resolution of
$\Delta m \sim 10^{-3}\,\Msun$.
We study the thermodynamic collapse, disk formation and fragmentation,
magnetic field amplification, and protostellar mass assembly in this
environment, providing the first cosmological RMHD simulation that
resolves individual Population III protostellar formation in a MW-progenitor halo.

The rest of the paper is organised as follows.
\autoref{sec:methods} describes the simulation setup, physics
modules, refinement strategy, and analysis methods.
\autoref{sec:results} presents the disk morphology, thermodynamic
and energetic evolution, disk stability, and magnetic field structure.
\autoref{sec:massevol} discusses the star formation history,
accretion rates, and protostellar mass function.
\autoref{sec:disc_comparison} compares our results to previous
Population III simulations.
We state our conclusions in \autoref{sec:conclusions}.


%% ============================================================
\section{Simulation and Methods}
\label{sec:methods}
%% ============================================================
\subsection{Simulation details}



For this paper we utilise the FORGE'd in FIRE framework of
\citet{FORGE_d_in_FIRE_Hopkin_2023}, extended to the primordial
star-formation regime similar to \citet{meziani26}.
We employ FIRE-3 \citep{FIRE_3_Updated_Hopkin} physics modules at
low resolution and STARFORGE \citep{STARFORGE_Towa_Grudic_2021}
physics to resolve individual protostellar formation. We briefly review these below and refer the reader to \citet{FIRE_3_Updated_Hopkin, FORGE_d_in_FIRE_Hopkin_2023, STARFORGE_Towa_Grudic_2021}. Numerical parameters not specified below are identical to those described in the above references.

\subsubsection{Code and gravity solver}
\label{sec:hydro}

The governing equations solved by the code are the ideal MHD equations
in conservative form \citep{A_new_class_of_Hopkin_2015, Hopkins_Raives_2015}:
\begin{equation}
	\frac{\partial \mathbf{U}}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = \mathbf{S},
	\label{eq:mhd_conservation}
\end{equation}
where the state vector of conserved quantities and the flux tensor are
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf{U} =
	\begin{pmatrix} \rho \\ \rho\mathbf{v} \\ \rho e \\ \mathbf{B} \end{pmatrix},
	\qquad
	\mathbf{F} =
	\begin{pmatrix}
		\rho\mathbf{v}                                                                     \\
		\rho\mathbf{v}\otimes\mathbf{v} + P_{T}\,\mathcal{I} - \mathbf{B}\otimes\mathbf{B} \\
		(\rho e + P_{T})\mathbf{v} - (\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{B})\mathbf{B}                 \\
		\mathbf{v}\otimes\mathbf{B} - \mathbf{B}\otimes\mathbf{v}
	\end{pmatrix}.
	\label{eq:mhd_flux}
\end{equation}
Here $\rho$ is the mass density, $\mathbf{v}$ the velocity,
$\mathbf{B}$ the magnetic field (in Gaussian units with $4\pi$ absorbed),
$e = u + |\mathbf{B}|^{2}/(2\rho) + |\mathbf{v}|^{2}/2$ the total specific energy,
$P_{T} = P + |\mathbf{B}|^{2}/2$ the total (thermal plus magnetic) pressure,
and $\mathcal{I}$ the identity tensor.
$\mathbf{S}$ includes source terms for self-gravity and the Powell divergence-cleaning
scheme \citep{Hopkins_Raives_2015} that maintains $\nabla\cdot\mathbf{B}\approx 0$.

The FORGE'd in FIRE framework is based on GIZMO Meshless Finite Mass (MFM) code
\citep{A_new_class_of_Hopkin_2015}.
It is a mesh-free Lagrangian Godunov method in which
the fluid equations are solved on a set of moving partition-generating points
whose masses are conserved exactly (no inter-particle mass flux), while
fluxes of momentum, energy, and magnetic flux are exchanged through
effective interparticle faces defined by a kernel-weighted partition of unity
applied to the particle distribution \citep{A_new_class_of_Hopkin_2015}.

Gravity is solved with the TreePM algorithm \citep{A_new_class_of_Hopkin_2015},
which combines a particle-mesh grid for large-scale forces with an oct-tree
for short-range forces \citep{Barnes_Hut_1986}, as in \citet{Springel_2010b}.
Gas particles use adaptive Lagrangian gravitational softening matching the
hydrodynamic kernel radius.
Sink/star particles use a fixed Plummer-equivalent softening
$\epsilon \approx 20$ AU.
Direct $N$-body summation is used for all sink--sink and sink--gas
gravitational interactions, with direct summation applied for
sink--sink separations below $1000\,\mathrm{AU}$ to accurately
resolve close binaries and dynamical ejections
\citep{STARFORGE_Towa_Grudic_2021}.



\subsubsection{Initial Conditions}
\label{sec:ics}

We use initial conditions (ICs) developed as part of the FIRE
collaboration \citep{FIRE_2}, specifically the m12f halo from the
Latte suite of Milky Way-mass zoom-in simulations
\citep{wetzel23}.
The m12f halo has a virial mass $M_\mathrm{vir} \sim 1$--$2 \times 10^{12}\,\Msun$
at $z = 0$ and was selected as an isolated system with a relatively
quiescent late-time merger history, making it a Milky Way analogue.
At the redshift of first collapse in our simulation ($z \sim 21$),
CDM part of the progenitor halo has a mass of $2 \times 10^{7}\,\Msun$,
typical of the minihaloes in which Population III stars are expected to form.
This value was obtained from running the ROCKSTAR halo finder
\citep{rockstar_behroozi12} on our snapshot.

We adopt pre-generated ICs at $z = 99$ in a $60\,\mathrm{Mpc}/h$ comoving
cosmological box.
The box contains a zoom-in region of comoving extent $\sim 3\,\mathrm{Mpc}/h$,
within which the FIRE galaxy forms at $z = 0$.
Only the zoom-in region contains baryonic particles and
high-resolution dark matter (DM) particles; the remainder of
the volume is populated with low-resolution DM particles.
As in the FIRE-2 Latte simulations, we adopt cosmological parameters
from WMAP-7 \citep{komatsu11}:
$h = 0.702$,
$\Omega_m = 0.272$,
$\Omega_\Lambda = 0.728$,
$\Omega_b = 0.0455$,
$\sigma_8 = 0.807$, and
$n_s = 0.961$

The full simulation snapshot contains $\sim 88$ million CDM particles
and FIRE-resolution gas particles whose smoothing lengths
($\sim$\,kpc) far exceed the protostellar disk scale.
We extract spatial cutouts centred on the most-massive sink particle
with a physical radius of $1 \kpc$ (22 ckpc).
The cutout sphere retains only the gas, high-resolution DM, and sink/star particles.
All analysis presented in this manuscript is of this cutout simulation.

Over the $\sim 15$ kyr of post-collapse evolution studied here, the
sound-crossing time across the cutout radius
($r_\mathrm{cut} \approx 1$ kpc) is
$\sim r_\mathrm{cut}/c_s \gtrsim 10$ kyr for $T \lesssim 10^4$ K,
comparable to or longer than our integration time.
The large-scale structure outside the cutout therefore has no causal
influence on the disk dynamics on these timescales, and the cutout
fully captures the relevant physics.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{profile_resolution.pdf}
	\caption{
		Top: Radial profile of gas particle mass (resolution).
		Scatter plot shows particle distribution at the time of first sink formation;
		solid line and shaded region correspond to the mean and $\pm 1\sigma$ for
		radial profiles across all snapshots after first sink formation.
		Horizontal lines correspond to $1/2 \Delta m$ of previous resolution region,
		since our particle splitting algorithm is subdividing parent particles in 2
		for each new level of split.
		Bottom: Same as Top, but for radial profile of equivalent cell size
		$\Delta x = (\Delta m / \rho)^{1/3}$.
	}
	\label{fig:profile_resolution}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Refinement strategy}
\label{sec:refinement}

We first evolve the full cosmological simulation with FIRE-3 physics until
first $10^5 M_\odot$ stellar population (SSP) particle forms.
We then restart from the preceding snapshot with an additional
``hyper-refinement'' layer applied globally \citep{FORGE_d_in_FIRE_Hopkin_2023}.

We refine gas cells (splitting into pairs of equal-mass daughter cells)
whenever their mass exceeds a fraction $f_J$ of the local Jeans mass:
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		\Delta m & > f_J M_J                                                                                                \\
		         & = f_J \frac{\pi}{6} \rho \lambda_J^3                                                                     \\
		         & = f_J \frac{\pi}{6} \rho \left( \frac{\pi c_s^2}{G \rho} \right)^{3/2}                                   \\
		         & = f_J \frac{\pi^{5/2}}{6} \left( \frac{k_B T}{\mu m_H G} \right)^{3/2} \rho^{-1/2}                       \\
		         & = f_J M_0 \left( \frac{T}{10^4 \text{ K}} \right)^{1.5} \left( \frac{n_H}{\text{cm}^{-3}} \right)^{-0.5}
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The last line follows from substituting the thermal Jeans length
$\lambda_J = (\pi c_s^2 / G\rho)^{1/2}$ and $c_s^2 = k_B T/(\mu m_H)$
into $M_J = (\pi/6)\rho\lambda_J^3$, and normalizing at
$T_0 = 10^4$ K and $n_0 = 1$ cm$^{-3}$.
We use $f_J = 10^{-2}$, corresponding to
$\sim 100$ resolution elements per Jeans mass and satisfying the
\citet{truelove97} criterion, since we resolve $100^{1/3} \approx 4.6 > 4$ cells
per Jeans length.
This is sufficient since MFM does not suffer from artificial fragmentation even
while refining \citep[in prep.]{khullar2026}. We also speculate that since MFM
is generally less diffusive than AMR, the more stringent criteria of 30 cells
per Jeans length needed to resolve effects like magnetic dynamo amplification
\citep{Federrath2011} does not necessarily apply here. Since our simulation
produces magnetic amplification by up to 4 orders of magnitude (see
\autoref{sec:bfield}), we leave detailed numerical checks to future work.

Only gas particles get split, DM particles stay at the same resolution.
This is a fine approximation because dark matter's potential is smooth at these
scales, and splitting of these particles should not change the results of our
simulation.

At our highest refinement level, the gas mass resolution is
$\Delta m \approx 10^{-3}\,\Msun$ and the spatial resolution is
$\Delta x \approx 2$ AU, sufficient to resolve densities
up to $n \sim 10^{15}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$.

\autoref{fig:profile_resolution} shows the radial profile of the
length scale of gas particles $\lambda = \left( m / \rho \right)^{1/3}$.
It exhibits the refinement profile, described in \autoref{sec:refinement}
at all times, except the earilest one.
Initial profile at $t-t_1=-0.5$ (0.5 kyrs before the first sink formation)
is  limited by the refinement scale inside of 100 AU radius (the center was not yet
refined to the highest resolution). But by the time of first sink
formation, the refinement reaches highest resolution that we expect from our
scheme, (around $10^{-3} M_\odot$ or a few AU) so the inner star forming disk
region is fully refined.

\subsection{Resolved physics}
%% ============================================================
\subsubsection{Magnetic fields}
\label{sec:mhd}

We integrate the ideal MHD equations as described in \citet{Hopkins_Raives_2015},
using the constrained-gradient method and divergence cleaning
\citep{Hopkins_2016_CG_MHD} for divergence control, which has been
validated for magnetorotational instability dynamics
\citep{FORGE_d_in_FIRE_Hopkin_2023}.

We include Spitzer--Braginskii thermal conduction and viscosity via
\citet{Hopkins_2017}. For metal-free gas at the temperatures
($T \sim 200$--$10^{4}$ K) and free-electron fractions relevant here,
these transport timescales exceed the local dynamical timescale by many
orders of magnitude \citep{meziani26}, so ideal MHD dominates throughout
the simulation.

The initial magnetic field is a trace cosmological seed field of
$|B_0| \sim 10^{-15}$ G
amplified self-consistently by gravitational compression,
differential rotation, and the turbulent dynamo.


%% ============================================================
\subsubsection{Radiation}
\label{sec:rad}

We solve radiation transport with the M1 moments method
\citep{Levermore_1984}, as implemented in {\small GIZMO} and described in
\citet{FORGE_d_in_FIRE_Hopkin_2023,STARFORGE_Towa_Grudic_2021, Hopkins_Grudic_2019, Hopkins2020}
across five frequency bands: H-ionizing, Lyman-Werner (LW)/photo-electric,
Near-UV (NUV), optical--NIR, and an adaptive-wavelength FIR band that tracks
the evolving dust emission temperature \citep{FIRE_3_Updated_Hopkin}.
Gas, dust, and radiation temperatures are evolved separately with
appropriate coupling terms, allowing self-consistent treatment of
both optically thin and optically thick regimes.
We use a reduced speed of light $\tilde{c} = 10^{-3} c$
following \citet{FORGE_d_in_FIRE_Hopkin_2023}.

The Lyman-Werner (LW) band is combined with the photo-electric (PE)
heating band into a single radiation frequency bin. This is justified
because both LW and PE photons originate from the same protostellar
sources and have similar mean free paths at the densities resolved here.
H$_2$ photo-dissociation by LW photons is accounted for through the
$\Gamma_{\mathrm{H_2}}$ destruction term in \autoref{eq:nh2}.

%% ============================================================
\subsubsection{Thermochemistry}
\label{sec:thermo}

We solve gas thermodynamics and chemistry as in \citep{FIRE_3_Updated_Hopkin}.
The following cooling functions are employed and are relevant to our setup:
$\Lambda_{\rm H}$ (atomic H cooling);
$\Lambda_{\rm HD}$ (HD cooling, \citealt{Galli_Palla_1998});
$\Lambda_{\rm H_2}$ (H$_2$ cooling);
$\Lambda_{\rm H_2}^{\rm thin},\, \Lambda_{\rm H_2}^{\rm thick}$
(H$_2$ interactions with $e^-,\,{\rm H}^+,\,{\rm H}_2,\,{\rm H},\,{\rm He}$
in optically thin and thick regimes).
We follow non-equilibrium H and He chemical networks.
Default FIRE-3 thermochemistry includes cooling due to metal lines, but we do
not evolve our simulation past the point of supernova-driven metal enrichment,
thus making all metal cooling contributions effectively zero (as expected in
the Population III formation regime).

Since we simulate metal-free primordial gas, the dust-to-gas ratio is zero
and the dust term in the H$_2$ formation equation is absent.
H$_2$ formation proceeds exclusively via gas-phase channels:
the two-body H$^{-}$ and H$_2^+$ pathways at low densities
($n \lesssim 10^8\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$) and the three-body
reaction (3H $\to$ H$_2$ + H) at high densities.

Molecular fraction of H$_2$ evolves in every cell according to (eq. 6 from
\citealt{FIRE_3_Updated_Hopkin}), with the dust formation term set to zero:
\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
		\frac{d \bar{n}_{\rm H_2}}{dt} ={} & \alpha_{\mathrm{GP}}C_2 \bar{n}_{\rm HI} \bar{n}_{\rm H} + \alpha_{3\mathrm{B}}C_3 \bar{n}_{\rm HI}\tfrac{ \bar{n}_{\rm H_2}}{8}\!\left( \bar{n}_{\rm HI}+\tfrac{ \bar{n}_{\rm H_2}}{8}\right) \\
		                                   & - \Gamma_{\mathrm{\rm H_2}} \bar{n}_{\rm H_2} - \xi_{\mathrm{\rm H_2}} \bar{n}_{\rm H_2} - \sum_{i}\beta_{\mathrm{\rm H_2},i}C_2 \bar{n}_{\rm H_2} \bar{n}_{i}
	\end{split}
	\label{eq:nh2}
\end{equation}

Here $\bar{n}_x$ is volume-averaged number density of $x$ inside of a cell.
$C_m = \left< n^m \right> / \left< n \right>^m$ is the $m^{\text{th}}$-order
clumping factor.
$\alpha_\mathrm{GP}$ is the gas-phase formation rate coefficient via the
H$^{-}$ and H$_2^+$ pathways \citep{Glover_Jappsen_2007,Abel_1997,Galli_Palla_1998}.
The three-body formation coefficient $\alpha_{3\mathrm{B}}$
\citep{abel02,Palla_1983} becomes the dominant H$_2$ formation channel
for $n \gtrsim 10^8\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$.
$\Gamma_{\mathrm{H_2}}$ is the coefficient associated with LW and ionizing photon
dissociation.
$\xi_{\mathrm{H_2}}$ accounts for cosmic-ray-induced ionization and dissociation
($\xi_{\mathrm{H_2}} \approx 3\times10^{-17}$ s$^{-1}$, the Milky Way value;
this rate is unconstrained at $z \sim 21$ and primarily affects H$_2$ formation
at $n \lesssim 10^7$ cm$^{-3}$, with negligible impact at the densities resolved here).
The last sum runs over collisional dissociation contributions from
$e^-, \rm H^+, H_2, HI, HeI, HeII$, with rate coefficients $\beta_{\rm H_2, i}$ from \citet{Glover_Abel_2008}.

Thermodynamic energy release from H$_2$ dissociation
($\sim 4.5$ eV per molecule) is not explicitly tracked in the gas energy equation.
This is acceptable because we do not have enough resolution to reach number
densities of $n \approx 10^{16} \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$, where H$_2$ dissociation
kicks in and becomes a non-negligible thermodynamic variable.
\citep{klessen_glover23} In addition, the
protostellar evolution tracks of \citet{Offner_2009} self-consistently evolve
the stellar radius without requiring the gas to track dissociation energy
explicitly.

At $z \sim 21$, the meta-galactic UV background \citep{Faucher-Giguere_2019} is
negligible compared to local protostellar radiation resolved in the simulation.
H$_2$ self-shielding against Lyman-Werner photons is included using the
shielding function of \citet{Gnedin_Draine_2014} (their Eq. 3), applied with a
local Sobolev column density $N\mathrm{H} \sim \rho / |\nabla \rho|$, with
turbulent line broadening accounted for following a Burgers velocity spectrum.

%% ============================================================
%\subsubsection{Cosmic rays}
%\label{sec:cr}


The simulation does not include cosmic-ray transport.
In our simulation, which covers only $\sim 15$ kyr of post-collapse
evolution and does not include supernovae, cosmic-ray injection from
stellar feedback is negligible; the initial cosmic-ray background
at $z \sim 21$ contributes subdominantly to H$_2$ dissociation and
ionization relative to the resolved radiation field.

%% ============================================================
\subsubsection{Stellar formation and evolution}
\label{sec:stars}

We resolve the formation of individual protostars in the densest region
of our simulation using the STARFORGE \citep{STARFORGE_Towa_Grudic_2021}
formation and evolution modules.
A gas particle becomes a sink (protostar) particle when
it meets the full STARFORGE formation criteria: the cell must be
(i) locally self-gravitating (virial parameter $\alpha_\mathrm{vir} < 1$),
(ii) Jeans unstable,
(iii) in a converging flow ($\nabla \cdot \mathbf{v} < 0$),
(iv) experiencing compressive tides in all directions,
(v) a local maximum of the density field, and
(vi) not within the accretion radius of a pre-existing sink
\citep{STARFORGE_Towa_Grudic_2021}.

Once formed, sink particles accrete bound gas whose circularised orbit falls
within the sink radius.
Each sink evolves along protostellar tracks following \citet{Offner_2009},
explicitly tracking the stellar radius, luminosity, and effective temperature
as a function of the instantaneous mass and accretion rate.
Protostellar jets are launched with a mass-loading proportional to the
surface accretion rate and a launch velocity comparable to the escape
velocity from the protostellar surface \citep{STARFORGE_Towa_Grudic_2021}.
\added{Jets are therefore expected in this simulation, but they are
	not resolved in the present run: a bug in the launch implementation
	suppresses them. We defer the analysis of jet feedback to future work.}


\subsection{Postprocessing methods}

\subsubsection{Disk identification}
\label{sec:diskid}

To compute velocity dispersion, local Toomre $Q$, and other disk
properties, we identify gas particles belonging to the disk at
each snapshot using the following procedure:

\begin{enumerate}
	\item We place the disk centre at the most massive sink
	      particle. This is more stable than the mass-weighted
	      centre-of-mass (COM) of all sinks once secondary sinks form
	      at large separations. Before any sinks form, the centre is
	      placed at the densest gas particle within a refinement region.
	\item We compute a mass-weighted COM velocity $v_\mathrm{COM}$ from gas particles within $r < r_\mathrm{search} = 10^{-5}\kpc \approx 2060\AU$ and subtract it from all particle velocities.
	\item We compute the disk normal $\Lhat$ from the
	      total angular momentum
	      $\mathbf{L} = \sum_i m_i\,(\mathbf{r}_i \times \mathbf{v}_i)$
	      summed over particles within $r_\mathrm{search}$, using the
	      COM-subtracted velocities from the previous step. The sign
	      of $\Lhat$ is fixed so that $L_z > 0$ to prevent
	      frame-to-frame flipping.
	\item We project particle positions and velocities onto the
	      disk-plane coordinate system
	      $(r_\mathrm{cyl},\,\phi,\,z,\,v_r,\,v_\phi,\,v_z)$.
	\item We apply geometric and density cuts:
	      $r_\mathrm{cyl} < r_\mathrm{max} = 10^{-5}\kpc$,
	      $|z|/r_\mathrm{cyl} < 0.3$ (aspect ratio), and
	      $\rho > \rho_\mathrm{thresh} = 10^{-15}\gcm$.
	\item We apply kinematic cuts:
	      $v_\phi > 0$ (co-rotating) and
	      $v_\phi / v_K > f_\mathrm{kep} = 0.3$, where
	      $v_K = \sqrt{G M_\mathrm{enc}(<r)/r}$ is the Keplerian speed
	      computed from the cumulative enclosed gas mass plus the total
	      sink mass placed at the origin.
	\item We extend the kinematically selected set to its bounding
	      cylinder (100th percentile in $r_\mathrm{cyl}$ and $|z|$) to
	      recover any disk material just outside the strict kinematic
	      boundary.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure*}[!htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Zoom1.png}
	\caption{Multi-scale density projection of the simulation at
		$t - \tform \approx 15 \kyr$, illustrating the dynamic range
		from the cosmic web (box size of $ \approx 100 \kpc$) to the center of the
		protostellar disk (resolving AU scales).
		White boxes mark the region shown in the next panel.
		White star symbols mark sink particles. }
	\label{fig:zoom}
\end{figure*}



Particles passing all cuts are labelled as disk members.
The disk radius $\rdisk$ is taken as the 90th-percentile
cylindrical radius of disk members.

%% ============================================================
\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}
%% ============================================================

In this section we present results from our cosmological RMHD simulation
of Population III star formation. We organize the discussion as follows:
\autoref{sec:collapse} examines the thermodynamic and energetic
evolution during the pre-stellar collapse and early disk phase;
\autoref{sec:disk} characterizes the disk structure and gravitational
stability; \autoref{sec:bfield} describes the magnetic field evolution;
and \autoref{sec:massevol} covers the star formation history, accretion
rates, and protostellar mass function.

\autoref{fig:zoom} presents a multi-scale density projection
of the simulation at $t - \tform \approx 15\kyr$, illustrating the
range of scales our simulation resolves after the onset of active
star formation.


\begin{figure*}[!htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{phase_combined.pdf}
	\caption{ Phase diagram (temperature and H$_2$ fraction vs.\ number density)
	at three representative epochs: pre-stellar ($t - \tform \approx -0.5\kyr$,
	left), first sink formation ($t - \tform = 0$, middle), and
	post-first-sink ($t - \tform = 1.2\kyr$, right).
	Green points correspond to disk-identified particles, red points correspond
	to points in our cutout simulation that have not been identified as disk,
	and blue points correspond to points in the full-box simulation right before
	the cutout was applied (only included in the leftmost plots). $f_{\rm H_2}=0$
	for most of particles in the low-density region (blue particles), thus missing from the bottom-left panel.
	\replaced{Solid black curves show the zero-metallicity
		Population~III collapse track (\citet{omukai00}, as digitized by
		\citet{klessen_glover23}) on the temperature (top) and H$_2$ fraction
		(bottom) panels. Coloured curves with shaded bands are binned medians of
		the simulated gas (the bands span $\pm 1\sigma$): blue is the
		instantaneous median at the pre-stellar epoch; dark red is the mean of the
		per-snapshot medians over $-0.5\,\mathrm{kyr} \le t - \tform \le 0$, and
		dark blue over $0 \le t - \tform \le 1.2\,\mathrm{kyr}$.}{The dashed curves in the temperature panels show the theoretical $T$--$n$
		tracks for primordial ($Z=0$) and metal-enriched ($Z=10^{-4}\,Z_\odot$) gas
		from \citet{klessen_glover23} (KG23); shaded bands indicate the median $\pm$
		standard deviation of the fits over the time intervals
		$-0.5\,\mathrm{kyr} \le t - \tform \le 0$ (middle) and
		$0 \le t - \tform \le 1.2\,\mathrm{kyr}$ (right), respectively.}}
	\label{fig:phases}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Collapse}
\label{sec:collapse}

The assembly of a primordial protostar proceeds through a sequence of thermally regulated stages: the gas cools via H$_2$ line emission until line optical depth and three-body reactions convert the flow into a fully molecular, opaque core, at which point compressional heating halts the collapse and a protostellar seed forms.
We examine the thermodynamic state of the infalling gas and the kinematics of the large-scale accretion flow that feeds the disk.

\subsubsection{Thermodynamics}
\label{sec:thermo}

The thermal and chemical state of the gas encodes the balance between the competing processes of compressional heating and molecular cooling.
The temperature--density phase trajectory is therefore a direct diagnostic of which physical process dominates at each stage of collapse, and $f_{H_2}$ molecular fraction phase plot shows the efficiency of molecular cooling. We include these
phase space plots in \autoref{fig:phases} for three representative epochs (pre-stellar,
first sink formation, post-first sink formation). Disk particles (identified by
the algorithm of \autoref{sec:diskid}) are marked in green, particles that
are a part of full simulation but not cutout are marked in blue, and particles
that are present in cutout simulation but not identified as disk particles are
marked in red.

At the pre-stellar epoch ($t - \tform = -0.5\kyr$; \autoref{fig:phases}, left
panels), the gas occupies a narrow track spanning $n \sim
	10^7$--$10^{10}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $T \sim 200$--$800$ K. This track traces
the initial slow collapse, during which H$_2$ ro-vibrational cooling brings the
gas down to the $\sim 200$ K temperature floor, followed by gradual
compressional heating as H$_2$ line cooling saturates and becomes optically
thick. The H$_2$ fraction (lower panel) rises modestly from $f_{\mathrm{H}_2}
	\sim 10^{-2.5}$ at $n \sim 10^7\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ to $f_{\mathrm{H}_2} \sim
	10^{-1.3}$ at $n \sim 10^{10}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$, driven primarily by two-body
formation channels (H$^-$ and H$_2^+$ pathways). Note that increase in H$_2$
fraction coincides with cooling of the gas, since H$_2$ is much more efficient
coolant compared to atomic H. The low-density gas particles ($n \leq 10^{-5}$
cm$^{-3}$) in the left column correspond to low-resolution gas in the original zoom-in box before re-simulation (blue markers). Most of those particles have
$f_{\rm H_2} = 0$, thus not present in the bottom plot.
This part of the simulation domain is outside our region of interest and does not change significantly over our integration time of $\approx 15 \, \rm kyr$.

The $T$--$n$ track at the moment of first sink collapse ($t - \tform = 0$)
follows the $Z = 10^{-4}\,Z_\odot$ metallicity track of
\citet{omukai00, klessen_glover23} rather than the zero-metallicity track.
We speculate that this slight offset may reflect small departures from
chemical equilibrium in the collapsing gas; we do not impose a metallicity
floor, so trace metal enrichment from surrounding regions cannot be ruled
out as a contributing factor. By the later epoch ($t - \tform = 1.2\kyr$),
the median of the phase distribution shifts back toward the $Z = 0$ track,
suggesting that the gas at this stage is governed more by primordial
H$_2$ chemistry. We defer a systematic one-zone test of this offset to
future work.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{energy_evolution.pdf}
	\caption{Top: Time evolution of the main energy components computed for
		disk-identified particles
		\added{(the disk radius $r_{\mathrm{disk}}$ is taken as the 90th-percentile
			cylindrical radius of disk-identified particles, see \autoref{sec:disk})}:
		rotational kinetic energy $E_\mathrm{rot}$ (blue),
		turbulent kinetic energy $E_\mathrm{turb}$ (green), total kinetic energy
		$E_\mathrm{kin} = E_\mathrm{rot} + E_\mathrm{turb}$ (black, dash-dotted),
		gravitational
		potential energy $|E_\mathrm{pot}|$ (red), thermal energy $E_\mathrm{therm}$
		(orange, dotted), and magnetic energy $E_\mathrm{mag}$ (magenta, dashed). \\
		Bottom: pure kinematic virial ratio
		$\alpha_{\text{kin}} = 2 E_\mathrm{kin}/|E_\mathrm{pot}|$
		(black); virial ratio with added thermal component (orange, dashed)
		$\alpha_{\text{kin+th}} = 2 \left(  E_\mathrm{kin} + E_\mathrm{therm} \right)/|E_\mathrm{pot}|$
		, and with added
		thermal and magnetic components (purple, dash-dotted),
		$\alpha_{\text{vir}} = 2 \left(  E_\mathrm{kin} + E_\mathrm{therm} + E_\mathrm{mag} \right)/|E_\mathrm{pot}|$
		. The dotted red line marks virial
		equilibrium ($= 1$). The disk starts sub-virial and reaches equilibrium by
		$\sim 2\kyr$.
		The inclusion of thermal energy reveals that the disk is thermally supported
		and near virial equilibrium at all times. Magnetic energy provides negligible
		additional support to the virial ratio. }
	\label{fig:energy_evol}
\end{figure}


By $t - \tform = 0$ (\autoref{fig:phases}, middle panels), the gas has
collapsed to much higher densities
($n \sim 10^{13}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$) and the thermal track has
extended significantly.
At intermediate densities ($n \sim 10^8$--$10^{10}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$),
compressional heating outpaces H$_2$ line cooling, which saturates
as the lines become optically thick, and the temperature rises
steadily through $T \sim 500$--$800$ K.
The H$_2$ fraction increases steeply in this regime as three-body
formation reactions (3H $\to$ H$_2$ + H) become efficient at
$n \gtrsim 10^8\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$, converting the gas to fully
molecular ($f_{\mathrm{H}_2} \to 1$) by
$n \sim 10^{11}$--$10^{12}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$.
Note that unlike in the pre-stellar regime, we see an increase in temperature
associated with increase in H$_2$ fraction. This is a second-order effect:
H$_2$ cools the gas very effectively, reducing thermal pressure, allowing the
gas to continue the infall process. This makes the gas opaque to H$_2$ lines,
heating it up. \citep{ripamonti_fragmentation_2004}


At $t - \tform = 1.2\kyr$ (\autoref{fig:phases}, right panels), the phase
diagram has broadened substantially. The gas has separated into 2 parts: a
continuously-infalling branch with $T < 2000$ K and $n < 10^{10}$ cm$^{-3}$,
and a hot branch at $T \sim 2000$--$8000$ K from shock-heated and adiabatically
compressed gas.
The H$_2$ fraction now shows a bimodality at high densities: gas that has been
heated to $T \gtrsim 2000$ K via radiation produced by the first formed sink
undergoes collisional H$_2$ dissociation.
We note that while our thermochemical model does not couple
H$_2$ dissociation to thermodynamics, the $\beta_{{\rm H}_2, i}$
terms in \autoref{eq:nh2} can still drive partial dissociation of H$_2$.

A low-$f_{\mathrm{H}_2}$ population
($f_{\mathrm{H}_2} \sim 10^{-3}$--$10^{-5}$) is produced at
$n \sim 10^{10}$--$10^{12}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$, while
the lower-density gas keeps following the infall trajectory, driven by 2-body
H$_2$ production pathway that the collapse started with (i.e. left panels).
\citep{klessen_glover23}
%The observed temperature floor at higher densities is a result of LW radiation
%produced by the first formed sink. In the surrounding lower-density
%gas, H$_2$ is photodissociated, removing the primary coolant and allowing
%compressional heating to maintain elevated temperatures. The disk itself,
%however, is dense enough for H$_2$ self-shielding: the high column density
%attenuates incoming LW photons before they can dissociate H$_2$ in the disk
%interior, allowing it to retain its molecular hydrogen and cool more
%efficiently to lower temperatures.

At later times, the three-body pathway to H$_2$ production becomes dominant way
of converting atomic hydrogen to molecular in collapsing regions. This results
in constantly increasing fraction of molecular hydrogen in the disk with time.


\subsubsection{Pre-stellar outside-in collapse}

A gravitationally supercritical core -- one in which the enclosed mass exceeds the local Jeans mass -- collapses outside-in: the outer shells fall inward faster than the inner ones because the excess of gravity over thermal pressure gradient increases with radius.
This contrasts with the inside-out expansion wave picture of \citet{Shu_1977}, which applies only when a core is marginally critical.
Here we characterize the accretion geometry and confirm that the simulated collapse is indeed driven from the outside in.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{profile_shell_mass_accretion.pdf}
	\caption{First plot: Radial density profiles through simulation time.
		The x-axis begins at $\sim 1\,\mathrm{AU}$, corresponding to the
		resolution limit of the simulation. The fit for the region outside of the disk
		is consistently $\rho \propto r^{-1.7}$ accross all times. \\
		Second plot: Gas mass in a ball vs radius of the ball before
		the formation of the first sink. The fit is roughly $M \propto r^1.3$.\\
		\replaced{Third plot: rate of change of the gas mass enclosed within a
			sphere of radius $r$, i.e.\ the integrated ``mass of the ball''
			accretion rate $\dot{M}(<r) = \mathrm{d}M(<r)/\mathrm{d}t$. At later times
			the inner region gains mass slower than at the eariler times
			(mostly close to the time of the first sink forming). There is a characteristic
			"flattening" at smaller radii inside of the disk before the first star has formed.
			Outer shells gain mass faster at all times -- a characteristic of "outside-in"
			collapse.}{Third plot: rate of change of gas mass in shell of radius $r$. At later times
			the inner region gains mass slower than at the eariler times
			(mostly close to the time of the first sink forming). There is a characteristic
			"flattening" at smaller radii inside of the disk before the first star has formed.
			Outer shells gain mass faster at all times -- a characteristic of "outside-in"
			collapse.}
		Smallest shells have a drop in $\dot{M}_{\text{shell}} $, associated with
		coming close to the resolution limit.\\
		Bottom plot: evolution of radial profile of kinematic virial parameter with
		time. The disk starts clearly sub-virial in the region inside of the disk
		($ < 10^{-2} $ pc), with virial parameter increasing by orders of magnitude
		at later times.
		\added{Note that this radial profile spans several orders of magnitude,
			whereas the global (mass-weighted) virial ratio in \autoref{fig:energy_evol}
			settles at $\sim 1$: the two probe different scales — the disk interior here
			versus the whole disk there.}}
	\label{fig:shell_mass_accretion}
\end{figure}


\begin{figure}[!htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{profile_density.pdf}
	\caption{Radial density profiles at three different times before the
		first bulk star formation event at $t-t_1 \approx 1.2\,\kyr$.
		The density increases with decreasing radius, and follows a power law
		relationship that steepens with time.}

	\label{fig:density_profile}
\end{figure}

We compute the gas mass in spherical shells
centered on the densest particle in the disk (which will later correspond
to the first sink particle location).
We perform this calculation for every snapshot, with a time interval
between snapshots of $\Delta t \approx 0.1\,\mathrm{kyr}$, and show the
shell mass profiles in the top panel of \autoref{fig:shell_mass_accretion}.
We show the rate of mass change per shell, $\Delta m / \Delta t$, in the bottom panel.

The fact that the outer layers gain mass faster demonstrates an
outside-in collapse. This contrasts with the inside-out model of
\citet{Shu_1977} and aligns with the collapse dynamics of gravitationally
unstable Bonnor-Ebert spheres
\citep{Ebert_1955, Bonnor_1956, Larson_1969, Penston_1969}.
\autoref{fig:kinematics_wide} confirms this: the infall velocity ($-v_r$) is
supersonic in the envelope outside the disk ($r > 2500$ AU), which is a
definitive signature of outside-in dynamics. Within the disk ($r < 2500$ AU),
the flow becomes predominantly subsonic. This abrupt deceleration occurs
because the infalling gas encounters an accretion shock as it strikes the
dense, rotationally supported disk material.




\subsection{Disk evolution}
\label{sec:disk}

Once the accretion flow acquires sufficient angular momentum to form a centrifugal barrier, a rotationally supported disk grows around the central protostar.
The subsequent evolution is governed by the competition between gravitational instability (which fragments the outer disk into secondary protostars), magnetic braking (which removes angular momentum and regulates the disk mass), and accretion-driven turbulence (which provides additional pressure support).
We characterize this evolution through the disk energetics, its morphology, and its gravitational stability.

\subsubsection{Energetics}
\label{sec:energyevol}

In this section, we consider the interplay of the gravitational, kinetic,
thermal and magnetic energies for the particles identified in the disk.
We compute the following energy components:
potential $E_{\text{pot}}$,
rotational $E_{\text{rot}}$,
turbulent $E_{\text{turb}}$,
thermal $E_{therm}$,
and magnetic $E_{\text{mag}}$, using Equations \ref{eq:Energies}:

\begin{subequations}
	\label{eq:Energies}
	\begin{align}
		E_\mathrm{pot}   & = -G \sum_{\substack{i < j                                     \\ i,j \in \mathrm{disk}}}
		\frac{m_i \, m_j}{\sqrt{|\mathbf{r}_i - \mathbf{r}_j|^2 + \epsilon^2}}            \\
		                 & -\; G \sum_{s \in \mathrm{sinks}} \sum_{i \in \mathrm{disk}}
		\frac{m_s \, m_i}{\sqrt{|\mathbf{r}_s - \mathbf{r}_i|^2 + \epsilon^2}}, \nonumber \\
		E_\mathrm{rot}   & = \tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{i \in \mathrm{disk}} m_i v_{\phi,i}^2,     \\
		E_\mathrm{turb}  & = \tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{i \in \mathrm{disk}} m_i |\mathbf{v}_i
		- v_{\phi,i}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}} - v_{r,i}\hat{\boldsymbol{r}}|^2,             \\
		E_\mathrm{therm} & = \sum_{i \in \mathrm{disk}} m_i u_i,                          \\
		E_\mathrm{mag}   & = \sum_{i \in \mathrm{disk}} \frac{|\mathbf{B}_i|^2}{8\pi}
		\frac{m_i}{\rho_i}
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}

where $i$ denotes all gas particles identified as disk members
(see \autoref{sec:diskid}),
$m_i$ is the mass of particle, $\textbf{v}_i$ is the total velocity vector,
$v_{\phi, i}, v_{r, i}$ are bulk velocity profile fits for azimuthal and radial
components for each annulus (both are functions of $r$ only),
$\textbf{B}_i$ is the magnetic field vector,
$\rho_i$ is the density, $u_i$ is the internal energy.

\autoref{fig:energy_evol} shows the time evolution of the main energy
components computed for disk-identified particles. Throughout the simulation,
$|E_\mathrm{pot}|$ dominates over all other components, confirming that the
disk is gravitationally bound. $E_\mathrm{rot}$ and $E_\mathrm{turb}$
are the main support terms and track each other to within a factor of $\sim 2$,
with $E_\mathrm{rot}$ slightly exceeding $E_\mathrm{turb}$. $E_\mathrm{therm}$
starts comparable to the kinetic energies at early times but becomes
subdominant at late times as the gravitational potential well deepens faster than the gas heats.

%(see \autoref{sec:diskid}).


The magnetic energy,
$E_\mathrm{mag}$, grows
over the $\sim 15\kyr$ simulation, from $\sim 10^{42}$ erg at
$t - \tform \approx -0.5\kyr$ to $\sim 10^{45.5}$ erg at $15\kyr$. This growth
is driven by a combination of flux-freezing compression during the collapse,
field-line winding
by differential rotation, and potentially the small-scale turbulent dynamo
\citep{sharda21}. Despite this rapid amplification, $E_\mathrm{mag}$ remains
below $E_\mathrm{therm}$ throughout the simulation, though the gap narrows at
late times -- consistent with the declining mass-to-flux ratios discussed in
\autoref{sec:bfield}.


The bottom panel shows the virial ratio $2 E_\mathrm{kin} / |E_\mathrm{pot}|$.
At early times ($t - \tform < 0$), the virial ratio is $\sim 0.6$--$0.7$,
indicating a \textit{sub-virial}, contracting state, i.e. the system has
insufficient kinetic energy to balance gravity.

The virial ratio rises as the disk forms and spins up, reaching $\sim 1.0$ by
$t - \tform \approx 2\kyr$. From $2$ kyr onward, the virial ratio oscillates
around unity, indicating that the disk has achieved and maintains approximate
virial equilibrium through a balance of rotational support, turbulent pressure,
and gravitational collapse. A dip in virial ratio after 11 kyr likely
corresponds to the slow-down of stellar accretion rate (when less gas
is accreted per unit time, less gravitational energy is converted to
kinetic energy, reducing the kinetic-to-potential ratio), which can also
be observed in \autoref{fig:mass_evol_rates}. The ``bounce-back" times also
align with the ``bounce-back" in the stellar accretion rate values.

The virial ratio never approaches the unbound threshold of $2$,
confirming that the system remains gravitationally bound throughout.


\subsubsection{Disk morphology and evolution}
\label{sec:morphology}

As the disk accretes mass and angular momentum, its morphology undergoes a characteristic sequence: an initially smooth, centrally concentrated structure gives way to spiral arms as the Toomre parameter drops below 1, and those arms subsequently fragment into secondary sinks.
The radial velocity structure simultaneously transitions from subsonic infall within the disk to supersonic infall in the surrounding envelope.
This signifies a presence of the accretion shock at the disk boundary.

