\subsubsection{Disk stability and fragmentation}
\label{sec:stability}

\skadd{In this section, we analyze the stability properties of the disk.}
Whether a self-gravitating disk fragments into bound clumps, or instead disperses the accumulated mass through spiral-arm shocks and turbulent transport depends on the ratio of stabilising pressures (thermal, turbulent) to the destabilising self-gravity of the gas. The Toomre parameter, defined in \autoref{eq:toomreq} \citep{Toomre_1964}, quantifies this balance locally: $Q < 1$ marks regions where perturbations on the disk scale grow faster than orbital shear can wind them up.
We track the spatial and temporal evolution of $Q$ to identify when and where fragmentation occurs, and how sink-driven velocity dispersion \skcomment{What does this term mean?} subsequently self-regulates the disk back toward marginal stability.

To characterize non-thermal motions in the disk, we decompose
particle velocities into radial and azimuthal components
$(v_r, v_\phi)$ in the disk-plane coordinate system.
We subdivide the disk into concentric cylindrical annuli and compute
the mass-weighted mean velocity in each annulus, yielding radial
profiles $\bar{v}_r(r)$ and $\bar{v}_\phi(r)$.

The residual (streaming-subtracted) velocity for each particle is
$\vec{\delta v} = (v_r - \bar{v}_r,\; v_\phi - \bar{v}_\phi,\; v_z)$.
The velocity dispersion $\sigma_v$ is computed as the root-mean-square
of $|\vec{\delta v}|$ within each annulus.

The Toomre $Q$ parameter quantifies the gravitational stability
of a differentially rotating disk:
\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:toomreq}
	Q = \frac{c_s \kappa}{\pi G \Sigma},
\end{equation}
where $c_s$ is the sound speed, $\kappa$ the epicyclic frequency,
and $\Sigma$ the surface density.

For the local Toomre $Q$ calculation we replace $c_s$ with
$\zeta = \sqrt{\sigma_r^2 + c_s^2}$, which includes both thermal
and turbulent contributions, since supersonic turbulence provides
additional effective pressure support.

For each snapshot the gas is binned into 20 annular bins \skcut{in the face-on frame}.
In each annulus $b$ we compute mass-weighted quantities:
\begin{equation}
	\Sigma_b = \frac{\sum_{i\in b} m_i}{\pi(r_{\mathrm{hi},b}^2 - r_{\mathrm{lo},b}^2)},
	\qquad
	\sigma_{r,b} = \sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i\in b} m_i\,v_{r,i}^2}{\sum_{i\in b} m_i} - \bar{v}_{r,b}^2}
\end{equation}
\skcomment{Make sure every single symbol is either has been described somewhere before the equation in the text or just after it. Like define $r_{\mathrm{hi},b}$ here.} \skcomment{The formula looks wrong. The first term is the same as the second term, right?} The angular velocity is measured directly from the rotation profile:
\begin{equation}
	\Omega_b = \frac{|\bar{v}_{\phi,b}|}{r_b}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{v}_{\phi,b}$ is the mass-weighted mean azimuthal velocity in annulus
$b$ and $r_b$ is the bin center. The epicyclic frequency is computed numerically
from the measured rotation curve via \citep{Pringle_King_2007}.
\begin{equation}
	\kappa_b^2 = \frac{2\,\Omega_b}{r_b}\,\frac{\mathrm{d}(r^2\,\Omega)}{\mathrm{d}r}\bigg|_{r_b}
\end{equation}
The Toomre parameter per annulus is then
\begin{equation}
	Q_b = \frac{\zeta_b \,\kappa_b}{\pi\,G\,\Sigma_b}
\end{equation}

A pixel-by-pixel face-on $Q$ map is produced by computing
local mass-weighted quantites for all of the relevant parameters:
\begin{equation}
	Q(x,y) = \frac{\zeta_b (x,y)\;\kappa(r_{x,y})}{\pi\,G\,\Sigma(x,y)}
\end{equation}
where $r_{x,y} = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$, and $\zeta = \sqrt{\sigma_r^2 + c_s^2}$,
which sums over thermal and turbulent contributions.

