\subsection{Magnetic fields}
\label{sec:bfield}

\skadd{In this section, we analyze the evolution of the magnetic field as the disk collapses and fragments.}
\skadd{We find that the} magnetic fields are amplified during the collapse by compression along field lines and by differential rotation within the disk once it forms.
The two relevant amplification channels -- flux freezing (compression-driven, $B \propto \rho^{2/3}$) and rotational winding (differential-rotation-driven, $B_\phi \propto r \partial\Omega/\partial r \cdot B_z$ \skcomment{The units don't look right, maybe you mean dB\_phi/dt?}) -- leave distinguishable imprints in the spatial structure and scaling of the field.
We characterize the resulting field morphology and its evolution through the vertical ($B_z$) and toroidal ($B_\phi$) components. \skcut{, and we quantify the coupling between matter and field through the mass-to-flux ratio \skcut{$\mu = M/\Phi_B$} \skadd{$\mu = M/M_\Phi$} normalized to the critical value.} \skcomment{Don't define stuff if you don't need it yet. Also whenever you write an equation, make sure the symbols have been defined either before the equation or just below it.}

%\subsubsection{Magnetic field structure}
%\label{sec:bfieldstruct}

Figures \ref{fig:combined_Btor}--\ref{fig:combined_Bz}
show face-on ($z=0$ slice) and edge-on ($x=0$ slice) views of the
toroidal and vertical components of the magnetic field at six epochs. \skcomment{A sentence on its own can technically be a paragraph, but shouldn't be one here. Why did you make slices instead of projections? Slices are more prone to stochastic effects. You could make this a projected average of the B field components.}

At the pre-stellar epoch, $B_z$ shows large-scale coherent structure
with a preferred sign, pointing towards primordial field orientation.
As the disk forms and develops spiral structure, $B_z$ is amplified
in the disk midplane and develops sign reversals that trace the
spiral arms, with field strengths reaching
$|B_z| \sim 10^{-2}$--$10^{-1}$ G at late times \skcomment{Why does the field change sign according to spiral arms?}.

The toroidal component $B_\phi$ (\autoref{fig:combined_Btor}) develops
an antisymmetric pattern by $t - \tform \approx 1.3\kyr$:
the field exhibits opposite signs on either side of the disk midplane,
as confirmed by the edge-on view.
This field can be achieved if we assume that primordial B-field
was directed in +x +y direction diagonally. \skcomment{I don't understand this last sentence and I don't understand why it should be opposite directions on either side of the disk midplane.}
By $t - \tform \approx 3.6\kyr$, $B_\phi$ displays a clear
two-armed spiral pattern with alternating positive and negative
regions, reaching strengths of $|B_\phi| \sim 10^{-1}$ G.
This antisymmetric, wound-up morphology is characteristic of field
amplification by differential rotation and is consistent with
a large-scale disk dynamo. By late times ($15\kyr$,) the toroidal
component of the field is directed mostly opposite with respect to the
direction of rotation of the disk.

% SPECULATION
Why did the toroidal field end up being anti-parallel to the direction of
rotation? A clue might be located in the fourth face-on plot,
corresponding to $\Delta t \approx 3.6\kyr$, roughly the time of first
bulk star formation event.
It is clear that at this state, the spacial distribution of the toroidal field
is evenly distributed between positive ($B_{tor} > 0$, red) and negative
($B_{tor} < 0$, blue) components, with each of two of spiral arms corresponding
to one of them. However, the spiral arm with denser gas corresponds to
$B_{tor} < 0$ regime, and the magnetic field is amplified in there during
collapse via flux-freezing mechanism. At later times this amplification is
dispersed arond the whole disk region, making $B_{tor} < 0$ regime dominant. \skcomment{I don't get what you're trying to say here in this paragraph, let's discuss this in our next meeting.}


\skcomment{You should always discuss the figures in order. Right now 14 is discussed before 13.}

We define mass-to-flux ration per annulus $b$ as:
\begin{equation}
	\mu_{\Phi,b} = \frac{2\pi\sqrt{G}\,\Sigma_b}{|B_{z,b}|}
\end{equation}

The mass-to-flux ratio $\mu_\Phi$ (\autoref{fig:profile_mf})
evolves significantly over time.

At early times ($t - \tform \leq 1.3\kyr$), $\mu_\Phi \sim 100$--$300$
at all radii, indicating an extremely magnetically supercritical
state in which gravity overwhelms magnetic support by more than
two orders of magnitude.
The mass-to-flux ratio then declines steadily as the field is
amplified by compression and winding:
by $t - \tform = 3.6\kyr$ (red), $\mu_\Phi$ has dropped to
$\sim 10$--$100$.
By $t - \tform = 6.3\kyr$ (purple), $\mu_\Phi$ exhibits large
radial scatter, with values ranging from $\sim 1$ to $\sim 100$
and dipping below the critical value ($\mu_\Phi < 1$) at some
inner radii.
At the latest epoch ($t - \tform = 9.5\kyr$, brown), significant
portions of the inner disk ($r \sim 200$--$500\AU$) have
$\mu_\Phi < 1$, indicating that the disk has become locally
\textit{magnetically subcritical} -- a state where magnetic
pressure and tension can in principle support the gas against
gravitational collapse.


This transition from $\mu_\Phi \sim 300$ to $\mu_\Phi \lesssim 1$
over $\sim 10\kyr$ represents a decrease of more than two orders
of magnitude and implies a corresponding amplification of the
magnetic field by flux-freezing compression, differential rotation
winding, and possibly the turbulent dynamo.

%\begin{figure*}[!htbp]
